WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
Hi team Wispr, I'm Sameep,

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:08.030
and I built my portfolio, sameep.ai.

3
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.130
It starts with a CD.

4
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.200
You'd mentioned you'd receive

5
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:17.300
2,000 of them when the post was live.

6
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.200
Also, I destroyed mine the way

7
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.220
you all destroyed the keyboard.

8
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.970
What you do is you fold it into a plane,

9
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.550
you throw it around, crumple it,

10
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.410
shred it, burn it,

11
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.430
and then it becomes a CD.

12
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.580
The CD opens a program which sort of

13
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.870
gives you the 1999 Mac vibe,

14
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.260
and you can obviously see all of my projects,

15
00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.400
but you also get a choose your own adventure

16
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.510
talk show podcast, so that you can

17
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.610
actually listen to me

18
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.510
talk to a host about the work that I've done,

19
00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.480
take you through some of the projects.

20
00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:59.380
And yeah, it was built almost entirely by voice.

21
00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.820
Four days, over 21 Claude sessions,

22
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.030
10,000 words, all dictated through Wispr.

23
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.060
I checked.

24
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.310
The real value wasn't just speed.

25
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.160
It was also being able to give

26
00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.070
a lot of context in one go.

27
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.490
Spoken on, you know,

28
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.910
spoken prompts carry all the edge cases.

29
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.770
And yeah, I think it also helps you

30
00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.780
review like a director without getting frustrated.

31
00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.300
And yeah, I could spin up a lot of sub-agents

32
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.360
because I was rambling on with my ideas,

33
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.100
you know, and different things were working,

34
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.010
happening in parallel.

35
00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.470
I also sort of gave that Easter egg

36
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.650
where voice can run the podcast itself.

37
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.400
Yeah, I'd love for you to try it out.

38
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.160
Thank you for the challenge.

39
00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:48.250
It was super fun.

40
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:49.780
Would love to work with you all.

